.TH CHOWN 1 
.SH NAME
chown, chgrp \- change owner or group
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chown
owner file ...
.PP
.B chgrp
group file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Chown
changes the owner of the
.I files
to
.IR owner .
The owner may be either a decimal UID or
a login name found in the password file.
.PP
.I Chgrp
changes the group-ID of the
.I files
to
.IR group .
The group may be either a decimal GID or
a group name found in the group-ID file.
.PP
Only the super-user can change owner or group,
in order to simplify as yet unimplemented accounting procedures.
.PP
.B Note:
that is enforced by the kernel and not by these programs.
.IR Chown (2)
here opens with
.PP
.RS
.B "if (!suser() || (ip = owner()) == NULL) return;"
.RE
.PP
\- kernel/sys4.c \- so an ordinary user is refused whatever the file's mode
says, and both programs are the super-user's alone.  Neither is
a set-user-ID program, and neither should become one: the whole point of the
rule is that a user cannot give a file away.
.PP
.B Note:
.I chgrp
has no manual page of its own and no source beyond cmd/chgrp/chgrp.c \- this
page documents both, the arrangement
.IR rm (1)
has with
.IR rmdir .
.PP
.B Note:
there is no
.IR chgrp (2).
The group is the third argument of
.IR chown (2),
which has no `leave this one alone' sentinel, so
.I chown
reads the group back out of a
.IR stat (2)
and writes it again, and
.I chgrp
does the same with the owner.  Each therefore rewrites the field it is not
changing, and a program that changes both must be prepared for either order.
.PP
.B Note:
the names available on the root filesystem this port ships are
.B root
(0),
.B daemon
(1),
.B sys
(2),
.B bin
(3),
.B uucp
(4) and
.B guest
(7); the groups are
.B other
(1),
.B sys
(2),
.B bin
(3) and
.B uucp
(4).  There is no group with gid 0, so
.B "chgrp 0"
must be written numerically.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's programs accepted an empty first argument as a number, so
.B "chown '' file"
handed the file to root without a word.  It is refused here.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Both exit
.B 4
for a usage error or an unknown name, and
.B 1
if any file could not be changed.  The first two messages go to the standard
.B output,
which is v7's and is inconsistent with the third; the third is
.IR perror (3)
and goes to the standard error.
.PP
.B usage: chown uid file ...
.br
.B usage: chgrp gid file ...
.PP
.BI "unknown user id: " name
.br
.BI "unknown group: " name
.PP
.BI name ": " reason
\- the file could not be
.IR stat ed
or
.IR chown (2)
refused it.  For an ordinary user the reason is
.BR "Not owner" .
.SH FILES
/etc/passwd
.br
/etc/group
.SH "SEE ALSO"
chmod(1),
chown(2),
ls(1),
passwd(5), group(5)
